=== step find
title: Find an element
minutes: 10
slot: js
--- explain
The browser turns your HTML into a tree of objects that JavaScript can read and change. This tree is called the **DOM** (Document Object Model). Every tag becomes an **element** object.

To get hold of one element, ask the `document` with a CSS selector, like the selectors you wrote on Day 2:

```js
const logo = document.querySelector(".logo");
logo.textContent = "TV Time";
```

- `document.querySelector(...)` gives back the **first** element that matches the selector, or `null` if nothing matches.
- `#` selects by id and `.` by class, as in CSS.
- `textContent` is the text inside the element. Change it, and the page changes straight away.

Keeping the element in a `const` means you can use it again and again without searching the page each time.

> Open the **index.html (whole page)** tab. Near the bottom is an empty `<div class="grid" id="grid">`. That's where the show cards will go.
--- task
Under the comment `Elements`:

1. Find the grid and keep it in a constant: `const grid = document.querySelector("#grid");`
2. As a test, put some text in it, for example "The shows will appear here."
3. Log the element with `console.log(grid);` and look at the Console under the preview.
--- hint
Three lines: the `const` with `querySelector`, then `grid.textContent = "...";`, then `console.log(grid);`. The id needs a `#` in front of it.
--- hint
```js
const grid = document.querySelector("#grid");
grid.textContent = "The shows will appear here.";
console.log(grid);
```
--- solution
// app.js: makes the ShowShelf page react (Day 7)
// Loaded before this file (open the read-only tabs to see them):
//   data.js    -> shows: the 12 shows
//   helpers.js -> ratingLabel, yearsLabel, searchByName, getByGenre, allGenres

// ---------- Elements: the parts of the page we change ----------
const grid = document.querySelector("#grid");
grid.textContent = "The shows will appear here.";
console.log(grid);
--- check
t.expect(/\b(const|let|var)\s+grid\s*=/.test(t.clean), "I can't find `const grid = ...` yet. Under the Elements comment, write `const grid = document.querySelector(\"#grid\");`.");
const grid = t.get("grid");
if (!grid || grid.id !== "grid") {
  t.expect(!/querySelector\(\s*["']grid["']\s*\)/.test(t.clean), "Your selector is `\"grid\"`, which looks for a `<grid>` tag. An id needs a `#` in front: `document.querySelector(\"#grid\")`.");
  t.fail("`grid` doesn't hold the grid element yet. Write `document.querySelector(\"#grid\")`, with the `#` and a capital S in `querySelector`.");
}
t.expect(t.text("#grid").length > 0, "The grid is still empty. Give it some text: `grid.textContent = \"The shows will appear here.\";`");
t.expect(t.logs.some((l) => /div#grid/.test(l.text)), "Now log the element: `console.log(grid);`. It appears in the Console under the preview.");
--- learned
`querySelector` finds an element in the page, and changing its `textContent` changes what people see. Everything today starts with finding an element.
--- summary
`document.querySelector("#id")` finds an element; `textContent` changes its text.
--- mistakes
- Forgetting the `#`: `querySelector("grid")` looks for a `<grid>` tag and gives `null`.
- `queryselector` or `QuerySelector`: capital letters matter.
- Writing `grid.textContent` before the `const grid` line.


=== step create
title: Build an element
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
JavaScript can also make **new** elements. It takes three moves: create it, fill it, put it on the page.

```js
const note = document.createElement("p");      // 1. create a <p> (not on the page yet)
note.className = "meta";                        // 2. give it a class...
note.textContent = "Updated today";             //    ...and some text
document.querySelector("footer").append(note);  // 3. put it inside the footer
```

- `document.createElement("p")` makes a new element. It exists only in JavaScript until you add it to the page.
- `className` sets the `class` attribute, so the CSS for that class applies to it.
- `parent.append(child)` puts `child` inside `parent`, after anything already there.

You can put elements inside elements you created, too: build the small parts first, then put them together.
--- task
1. Delete the `grid.textContent = ...` line from step 1. The `console.log` can go too.
2. Under `const grid`, create an `article` element with the class `card`.
3. Create an `h3` whose text is the first show's name: `shows[0].name`.
4. Put the `h3` inside the article, and the article inside the grid.

A small white card with "Friends" appears in the preview.
--- hint
Two `document.createElement(...)` calls: one for `"article"`, one for `"h3"`. Then two `append` calls: first `card.append(title)`, then `grid.append(card)`.
--- hint
```js
const card = document.createElement("article");
card.className = "card";

const title = document.createElement("h3");
title.textContent = shows[0].name;

card.append(title);
grid.append(card);
```
--- solution
// app.js: makes the ShowShelf page react (Day 7)
// Loaded before this file (open the read-only tabs to see them):
//   data.js    -> shows: the 12 shows
//   helpers.js -> ratingLabel, yearsLabel, searchByName, getByGenre, allGenres

// ---------- Elements: the parts of the page we change ----------
const grid = document.querySelector("#grid");

// ---------- A first card ----------
const card = document.createElement("article");
card.className = "card";

const title = document.createElement("h3");
title.textContent = shows[0].name;

card.append(title);
grid.append(card);
--- check
const card = t.$("#grid article");
if (!card) {
  t.expect(!t.$("#grid h3"), "Your `h3` is in the grid, but not inside an `article`. Append the `h3` to the card, and then the card to the grid.");
  t.fail("I can't find an `<article>` in the grid yet. Create it with `document.createElement(\"article\")` and add it with `grid.append(card);`.");
}
t.expect(card.classList.contains("card"), "Give the article the class `card`: `card.className = \"card\";`.");
const h3 = card.querySelector("h3");
t.expect(h3, "Put an `h3` inside the card: create it with `document.createElement(\"h3\")`, then `card.append(title);`.");
const names = t.get("shows").map((s) => s.name);
t.expect(names.includes(t.text(h3)), `The heading in the card says "${t.text(h3)}". Use the first show's name: \`title.textContent = shows[0].name;\``);
const leftover = [...t.$("#grid").childNodes].some((n) => n.nodeType === 3 && n.textContent.trim());
t.expect(!leftover, "The test text from step 1 is still in the grid. Delete the line `grid.textContent = ...`.");
--- learned
You built part of the page with JavaScript: `createElement`, fill it, `append`. The CSS for `.card` applied straight away, because you gave it the class.
--- summary
`document.createElement("tag")` makes an element; `parent.append(child)` puts it on the page.
--- mistakes
- `card.class = "card"`: the property is called `className`.
- Appending the card to the grid before putting the title in it (it works, but it's easier to read in the other order).
- Forgetting `grid.append(card)`, so the card never appears.


=== step create-card
title: A function that builds a card
minutes: 20
slot: js
--- explain
You'll need a card for every show, so the card code belongs in a **function**. We've moved your code from step 2 into a function called `createCard(show)` and added the other parts of a card: a poster, a genre badge, the rating and the years.

Read it from top to bottom:

- it gets one show object as its parameter, `show`
- it creates elements and fills them from `show`
- `append` can take several elements at once: `body.append(badge, title, rating, meta)` adds all four, in that order
- at the end, `return card;` gives the finished card back. The function doesn't put it on the page: the last line does that.

Three lines are still missing. They are marked `// TODO`.

To join pieces of text, use `+`:

```js
label.textContent = "Season " + season.number + " of " + total;
```

> `show.image ?? "poster.svg"` means "the show's picture, or `poster.svg` if it has none (`null`)". Real pictures arrive tomorrow.
--- task
Fill in the three TODO lines inside `createCard`, using the helpers from **helpers.js**:

1. The badge shows the first genre: `show.genres[0]`.
2. The rating line reads like `★ 8.8 · Must watch`: the rating, then the label from `ratingLabel(show.rating)`. Copy the `★` and `·` from the comment, or use `*` and `-`.
3. The meta line shows the years: `yearsLabel(show.premiered, show.ended)`.
--- starter
// app.js: makes the ShowShelf page react (Day 7)
// Loaded before this file (open the read-only tabs to see them):
//   data.js    -> shows: the 12 shows
//   helpers.js -> ratingLabel, yearsLabel, searchByName, getByGenre, allGenres

// ---------- Elements: the parts of the page we change ----------
const grid = document.querySelector("#grid");

// ---------- One card for one show ----------
// createCard(show) builds a card and gives it back. It doesn't put it on the page.
function createCard(show) {
  const card = document.createElement("article");
  card.className = "card";

  const poster = document.createElement("img");
  poster.className = "poster";
  poster.src = show.image ?? "poster.svg";   // no picture (null)? use poster.svg
  poster.alt = "";                           // the name is under the poster, so no alt text

  const body = document.createElement("div");
  body.className = "card-body";

  const badge = document.createElement("span");
  badge.className = "badge";
  // TODO 1: the badge shows the first genre (show.genres[0])

  const title = document.createElement("h3");
  title.textContent = show.name;

  const rating = document.createElement("p");
  rating.className = "rating";
  // TODO 2: the rating, like "★ 8.8 · Must watch" (show.rating and ratingLabel)

  const meta = document.createElement("p");
  meta.className = "meta";
  // TODO 3: the years, like "1994–2004" (yearsLabel with show.premiered and show.ended)

  body.append(badge, title, rating, meta);
  card.append(poster, body);
  return card;
}

// Try it with the first show
grid.append(createCard(shows[0]));
--- hint
Each TODO is one line that sets `textContent`, like the title line above it: `title.textContent = show.name;`. For the rating, join the pieces with `+`.
--- hint
```js
badge.textContent = show.genres[0];
```
```js
rating.textContent = "★ " + show.rating + " · " + ratingLabel(show.rating);
```
```js
meta.textContent = yearsLabel(show.premiered, show.ended);
```
--- solution
// app.js: makes the ShowShelf page react (Day 7)
// Loaded before this file (open the read-only tabs to see them):
//   data.js    -> shows: the 12 shows
//   helpers.js -> ratingLabel, yearsLabel, searchByName, getByGenre, allGenres

// ---------- Elements: the parts of the page we change ----------
const grid = document.querySelector("#grid");

// ---------- One card for one show ----------
// createCard(show) builds a card and gives it back. It doesn't put it on the page.
function createCard(show) {
  const card = document.createElement("article");
  card.className = "card";

  const poster = document.createElement("img");
  poster.className = "poster";
  poster.src = show.image ?? "poster.svg";   // no picture (null)? use poster.svg
  poster.alt = "";                           // the name is under the poster, so no alt text

  const body = document.createElement("div");
  body.className = "card-body";

  const badge = document.createElement("span");
  badge.className = "badge";
  badge.textContent = show.genres[0];

  const title = document.createElement("h3");
  title.textContent = show.name;

  const rating = document.createElement("p");
  rating.className = "rating";
  rating.textContent = "★ " + show.rating + " · " + ratingLabel(show.rating);

  const meta = document.createElement("p");
  meta.className = "meta";
  meta.textContent = yearsLabel(show.premiered, show.ended);

  body.append(badge, title, rating, meta);
  card.append(poster, body);
  return card;
}

// Try it with the first show
grid.append(createCard(shows[0]));
--- check
const createCard = t.get("createCard");
t.expect(typeof createCard === "function", "I can't find the function `createCard`. Press **Start again** to get the code back.");
const shows = t.get("shows");
let card;
try { card = createCard(shows[1]); } catch (e) { t.fail("Your `createCard` crashed: " + e.message); }
t.expect(card && card.nodeType === 1, "`createCard` should give the card back. Keep `return card;` as its last line.");
const badge = card.querySelector(".badge");
const bt = badge ? badge.textContent.trim() : "";
t.expect(bt !== "", "TODO 1: the badge is empty. Set its text to the first genre: `badge.textContent = show.genres[0];`");
t.expect(bt === "Drama", `TODO 1: for Breaking Bad the badge says "${bt}". It should show only the first genre, "Drama": use \`show.genres[0]\`.`);
const rt = t.text(card.querySelector(".rating"));
t.expect(rt !== "", "TODO 2: the rating line is empty. Set `rating.textContent` to the rating and its label.");
t.expect(rt.includes("9.2"), `TODO 2: for Breaking Bad the rating line says "${rt}". It should contain the number 9.2, which is \`show.rating\`.`);
t.expect(/must watch/i.test(rt), `TODO 2: the rating line says "${rt}". Add the label from \`ratingLabel(show.rating)\`, which gives "Must watch" for 9.2.`);
const mt = t.text(card.querySelector(".meta"));
t.expect(mt.includes("2008") && mt.includes("2013"), `TODO 3: for Breaking Bad the meta line says "${mt}". It should show the years 2008–2013: use \`yearsLabel(show.premiered, show.ended)\`.`);
t.expect(t.$$("#grid .card").length >= 1, "Keep the last line, `grid.append(createCard(shows[0]));`, so a card appears on the page.");
--- learned
`createCard` turns one show object into one card element. A function that **returns** an element is a small factory: give it any show, and it builds that show's card.
--- summary
A function can build an element from an object and `return` it.
--- mistakes
- `badge.textContent = show.genres;` shows every genre with commas. Use `[0]` for the first one.
- Missing `+` between the pieces of the rating text, which gives a syntax error.
- Deleting `return card;`, so `createCard` gives back `undefined`.
--- teacher
The code box starts fresh in this step: their step 2 lines were moved into the function. If a student asks where their code went, show them the `card` and `title` lines at the top of `createCard`.


=== step loop
title: A card for every show
minutes: 10
slot: js
--- explain
You know `for...of` from earlier days: it runs the same code once for every item in an array.

```js
for (const name of ["Dark", "Fleabag"]) {
  console.log("Now watching " + name);
}
```

Now put your function inside a loop: for each show, create a card and append it to the grid.

This is the heart of building pages with JavaScript: **data in, elements out**. One function describes what a single card looks like; the loop makes as many cards as there are shows. Add a thirteenth show to the data, and a thirteenth card appears, with no new HTML.
--- task
Replace the last line, `grid.append(createCard(shows[0]));`, with a `for...of` loop that adds a card for **every** show in `shows`.
--- hint
Loop over `shows` with a variable called `show`. Inside the loop, the same line as before, but with `show` instead of `shows[0]`.
--- hint
```js
for (const show of shows) {
  grid.append(createCard(show));
}
```
--- solution-replace
// Try it with the first show
grid.append(createCard(shows[0]));
=>
// One card for every show
for (const show of shows) {
  grid.append(createCard(show));
}
--- check
const cards = t.$$("#grid .card");
t.expect(cards.length > 1, "There's only one card. Replace the last line with a `for...of` loop over `shows` that appends `createCard(show)` for each one.");
t.expect(cards.length !== 13, "There are 13 cards: Friends is there twice. Delete the old line `grid.append(createCard(shows[0]));`.");
t.expect(cards.length === 12, `There are ${cards.length} cards. There should be 12, one per show.`);
const names = cards.map((c) => t.text(c.querySelector("h3")));
t.expect(new Set(names).size === 12, "The same show appears on every card. Inside the loop, use the loop variable: `createCard(show)`, not `createCard(shows[0])`.");
--- learned
Twelve cards from three lines of code. If the data changes, the page follows, with no new HTML.
--- summary
A loop plus a function: one element for every item in an array.
--- mistakes
- Keeping the old line, so Friends appears twice.
- `createCard(shows)` (the whole array) instead of `createCard(show)`.


=== step render-grid
title: Draw any list of shows
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
Soon the grid will change while you use the page: fewer cards while you search, all of them again afterwards. So the drawing code has to run again and again, with different lists. That's a job for a function with a parameter:

```js
function renderGrid(list) { ... }

renderGrid(shows);                        // all 12
renderGrid(searchByName(shows, "the"));   // only the matching shows
```

One thing to watch: `append` adds to what is already there. Draw twice and you'd have 24 cards. So the function must first empty the grid:

```js
grid.replaceChildren();   // removes everything inside the grid
```

Then it loops over `list`, its parameter, and not over `shows`. That way it draws whatever list it's given.

> "Render" is the word developers use for "draw on the screen".
--- task
1. Put your loop inside a new function `renderGrid(list)`. Make `grid.replaceChildren();` its first line, and loop over `list`.
2. Under the function, call it with all the shows: `renderGrid(shows);`
--- hint
The function has two parts: first `grid.replaceChildren();`, then your loop with `list` where it said `shows`.
--- hint
```js
function renderGrid(list) {
  grid.replaceChildren();
  for (const show of list) {
    grid.append(createCard(show));
  }
}

renderGrid(shows);
```
--- solution-replace
// One card for every show
for (const show of shows) {
  grid.append(createCard(show));
}
=>
// ---------- Draw a list of shows in the grid ----------
function renderGrid(list) {
  grid.replaceChildren();   // remove the old cards first
  for (const show of list) {
    grid.append(createCard(show));
  }
}

renderGrid(shows);
--- check
const renderGrid = t.get("renderGrid");
t.expect(typeof renderGrid === "function", "I can't find a function called `renderGrid`. Wrap your loop in `function renderGrid(list) { ... }`.");
t.expect(t.$$("#grid .card").length === 12, "The page should start with all 12 cards. Under the function, call it: `renderGrid(shows);`.");
const shows = t.get("shows");
try { renderGrid([shows[2], shows[5]]); } catch (e) { t.fail("Your `renderGrid` crashed: " + e.message); }
const n = t.$$("#grid .card").length;
t.expect(n !== 14, "`renderGrid` added the new cards after the old ones (14 cards). Empty the grid first: `grid.replaceChildren();` as the first line of the function.");
t.expect(n !== 12 && n !== 24, "When I gave `renderGrid` a list of two shows, it still drew all 12. Loop over the parameter `list`, not over `shows`.");
t.expect(n === 2, `When I gave \`renderGrid\` a list of two shows, it drew ${n} cards. It should draw exactly the shows in \`list\`.`);
const names = t.$$("#grid .card h3").map((h) => t.text(h));
t.expect(names[0] === "The Office" && names[1] === "Dark", "The cards should follow the order of the list you give to `renderGrid`.");
renderGrid(shows);
--- learned
`renderGrid(list)` draws any list you give it, and starts from an empty grid every time. Next, you'll call it while someone types.
--- summary
`renderGrid(list)` empties the grid with `replaceChildren()` and draws a card for each show.
--- mistakes
- Forgetting `grid.replaceChildren()`, so cards pile up.
- Looping over `shows` inside the function, so the parameter is ignored.


=== step search-input
title: React to typing
minutes: 15
slot: js
--- explain
An **event** is something that happens on the page: a click, a key press, the text in a box changing. You can ask an element to run a function whenever an event happens:

```js
const logo = document.querySelector(".logo");
logo.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Someone clicked the logo");
});
```

- `"click"` is the name of the event.
- The arrow function is the **event listener**. The browser calls it every time the event happens, not now.

For a text box, the `"input"` event happens on every change: each letter typed or deleted. Inside the listener, `searchInput.value` is the text that is in the box right now.

You also have `searchByName(list, text)` in helpers.js. It gives back only the shows whose name contains the text, whatever the letter case.
--- task
1. At the top, under `const grid`, find the search box: `const searchInput = document.querySelector("#search");`
2. At the bottom, add an `"input"` listener to `searchInput`. Inside it, draw only the matching shows: `renderGrid(searchByName(shows, searchInput.value));`

Then type **office** in the search box in the preview, and delete it again.
--- hint
The listener is `searchInput.addEventListener("input", () => { ... });`. Inside the curly brackets goes one line: the `renderGrid(...)` call.
--- hint
```js
searchInput.addEventListener("input", () => {
  renderGrid(searchByName(shows, searchInput.value));
});
```
--- solution-replace
const grid = document.querySelector("#grid");
=>
const grid = document.querySelector("#grid");
const searchInput = document.querySelector("#search");
--- solution-append
// ---------- Events: what happens when someone uses the page ----------
// Every time the text in the search box changes, show only the shows that match
searchInput.addEventListener("input", () => {
  renderGrid(searchByName(shows, searchInput.value));
});
--- check
await t.sleep(450);
await t.rerun();   // start from a fresh run of the page
const input = t.$("#search");
const typeIn = async (v) => { input.value = v; input.dispatchEvent(new t.win.Event("input", { bubbles: true })); await t.sleep(80); };
const names = () => t.$$("#grid .card h3").map((h) => t.text(h));
t.expect(names().length === 12, "The page should start with all 12 cards. Keep `renderGrid(shows);` under the function, and look in the Console for an error.");
await typeIn("office");
if (names().length === 12) {
  t.expect(!/addEventListener\(\s*["']change["']/.test(t.clean), "Use the `\"input\"` event. `\"change\"` only happens when you leave the box, not while you type.");
  t.expect(/\b(const|let)\s+searchInput\s*=/.test(t.clean), "At the top, find the search box: `const searchInput = document.querySelector(\"#search\");`.");
  t.fail("Typing **office** in the search box didn't change the grid. Add an `\"input\"` listener to `searchInput` that calls `renderGrid(searchByName(shows, searchInput.value));`.");
}
t.expect(names().length === 1 && names()[0] === "The Office", `After typing "office" the grid shows: ${names().join(", ") || "nothing"}. It should show only The Office. Use \`searchInput.value\`, the text in the box.`);
await typeIn("");
t.expect(names().length === 12, "After emptying the search box, all 12 shows should come back.");
await typeIn("BAD");
t.expect(names().length === 1 && names()[0] === "Breaking Bad", "Searching BAD in capitals should find Breaking Bad. Use `searchByName`, which ignores letter case.");
await typeIn("");
--- learned
`addEventListener` runs your code **later**, whenever the event happens. The page now reacts to the person using it, which is what makes it an app.
--- summary
`element.addEventListener("input", () => { ... })` runs code on every change; `.value` is the text in a box.
--- mistakes
- `searchInput.value` read once outside the listener: it's empty at that moment and never updates.
- Using the `"change"` event, which waits until you leave the box.
- Calling the function instead of giving it: `addEventListener("input", renderGrid(...))`.


=== step submit
title: Stop the page reloading
minutes: 10
slot: js
--- explain
Try it: click in the search box in the preview and press **Enter**. The Lab shows a notice: the form tried to reload the page.

That's what a `<form>` does when it is **submitted** (with Enter, or its Search button): it sends the text to a server and loads a new page. We don't have a server, and we want to stay on this page.

Every listener receives an **event object** with details about what happened. Its method `preventDefault()` cancels what the browser would normally do:

```js
link.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();   // don't follow the link
});
```

The event for a form is `"submit"`. It happens on the `<form>` itself, not on the button or the text box.
--- task
1. At the top, find the form: `const searchForm = document.querySelector("#search-form");`
2. At the bottom, add a `"submit"` listener to `searchForm` that calls `event.preventDefault();`.

Press Enter in the search box again: no notice this time.
--- hint
The arrow function needs a parameter this time, `(event) => { ... }`, so you can use `event.preventDefault();` inside it.
--- hint
```js
searchForm.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
});
```
--- solution-replace
const searchInput = document.querySelector("#search");
=>
const searchInput = document.querySelector("#search");
const searchForm = document.querySelector("#search-form");
--- solution-append
// Pressing Enter sends ("submits") the form. Stop the browser from reloading the page.
searchForm.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
});
--- check
await t.sleep(450);
await t.rerun();   // start from a fresh run of the page
const form = t.$("#search-form");
let prevented = null;
const spy = (e) => { prevented = e.defaultPrevented; };
t.doc.addEventListener("submit", spy);
form.dispatchEvent(new t.win.Event("submit", { bubbles: true, cancelable: true }));
t.doc.removeEventListener("submit", spy);
if (!prevented) {
  t.expect(!/\.preventDefault\s*;/.test(t.clean), "`preventDefault` is a method: call it with brackets, `event.preventDefault();`.");
  t.expect(!/searchInput\.addEventListener\(\s*["']submit/.test(t.clean), "The `\"submit\"` event happens on the form, not on the text box. Use `searchForm.addEventListener(\"submit\", ...)`.");
  t.fail("The form still tries to reload the page. Add a `\"submit\"` listener to `searchForm` that calls `event.preventDefault();`.");
}
t.expect(t.$$("#grid .card").length === 12, "The grid should still show all 12 shows. Look in the Console for an error.");
--- learned
Listeners get an **event object**. `event.preventDefault()` stops the browser's normal behaviour, here the page reload. You'll use it for every form.
--- summary
`event.preventDefault()` stops the browser's default action, like a form reloading the page.
--- mistakes
- Adding the listener to the button with `"click"`: Enter in the box still submits the form.
- `event.preventDefault` without brackets: nothing happens.
- Forgetting the `event` parameter: `event is not defined`.


=== step empty
title: When nothing matches
minutes: 10
slot: js
--- explain
Search for something that doesn't exist, like **zzz**. The grid goes empty, and the page looks broken. It's better to say what happened.

The page already has a message for this, hidden: `<p class="status" id="empty-message" hidden>`. Every element has a `hidden` property: `true` hides it, `false` shows it.

```js
banner.hidden = true;    // hide it
banner.hidden = false;   // show it
```

A comparison like `list.length > 0` is already `true` or `false`, so you can use it directly, without an `if`:

```js
saleBanner.hidden = itemsOnSale.length === 0;   // hide the banner when nothing is on sale
```
--- task
1. At the top, find the message: `const emptyMessage = document.querySelector("#empty-message");`
2. At the end of `renderGrid`, add one line that hides the message when the list has shows, and shows it when the list is empty.

Search for zzz, then for dark.
--- hint
Inside `renderGrid`, after the loop: `emptyMessage.hidden = ...;`. The message should be hidden when there **are** shows, so when `list.length > 0`.
--- hint
```js
function renderGrid(list) {
  grid.replaceChildren();
  for (const show of list) {
    grid.append(createCard(show));
  }
  emptyMessage.hidden = list.length > 0;
}
```
--- solution-replace
const searchForm = document.querySelector("#search-form");
=>
const searchForm = document.querySelector("#search-form");
const emptyMessage = document.querySelector("#empty-message");
--- solution-replace
    grid.append(createCard(show));
  }
}
=>
    grid.append(createCard(show));
  }
  emptyMessage.hidden = list.length > 0;   // show the message only when there are no shows
}
--- check
await t.sleep(450);
await t.rerun();   // start from a fresh run of the page
const msg = t.$("#empty-message");
const input = t.$("#search");
const typeIn = async (v) => { input.value = v; input.dispatchEvent(new t.win.Event("input", { bubbles: true })); await t.sleep(80); };
t.expect(t.$$("#grid .card").length === 12, "The page should start with all 12 cards. Look in the Console: if it says \"Cannot access 'emptyMessage' before initialization\", move `const emptyMessage = ...` to the top, under `const grid`.");
t.expect(msg.hidden, "With all 12 shows on the page, the message should be hidden. It should be hidden when there are shows: `emptyMessage.hidden = list.length > 0;`.");
await typeIn("zzz");
t.expect(t.$$("#grid .card").length === 0, "Searching for zzz should leave the grid empty. Keep your search listener from step 6.");
t.expect(!msg.hidden, "When I search for zzz, the grid is empty but the message stays hidden. At the end of `renderGrid`, add `emptyMessage.hidden = list.length > 0;`.");
await typeIn("dark");
t.expect(msg.hidden, "When I search for dark, there's a result, but the message is still showing. It should be hidden when `list.length > 0`.");
await typeIn("");
--- learned
`element.hidden = true/false` shows and hides things. Setting it from a comparison keeps the message and the grid in agreement every time the grid is drawn.
--- summary
`element.hidden = condition;` shows or hides an element.
--- mistakes
- The condition the wrong way round: `list.length === 0` hides the message exactly when it's needed.
- Putting the line outside `renderGrid`, so it only runs once.
- `const emptyMessage` at the bottom of the file: "Cannot access before initialization".


=== step genres
title: Genre buttons and one render()
minutes: 30
slot: js
--- explain
Now we add a button per genre (called a **chip**). But careful: the grid now depends on two things, the search text **and** the genre. If the search box and the chips each drew the grid their own way, they would undo each other's work.

The fix is a pattern you'll use in every app from now on:

1. Keep everything that can change in one object, the **state**.
2. Write one function, `render()`, that draws the page from the state.
3. Events only change the state, and then call `render()`.

```js
state.genre = "Comedy";   // something happened: change the state
render();                 // draw the page again from the state
```

We've added the `state` object, `render()` and `renderGenres()` below your code. `renderGenres()` makes one chip per genre with `allGenres(shows)`, and gives the chosen one the class `active`.

> `""` (an empty text) in `state.genre` means "no genre chosen: show all genres".
--- task
1. **TODO 1** in `render()`: if `state.genre` is not `""`, keep only that genre, with `getByGenre(list, state.genre)`.
2. **TODO 2** in the chip's click listener: if `state.genre` is already this `genre`, set it to `""`; otherwise set it to `genre`. Then call `render()`.
3. In your search listener, replace the `renderGrid(...)` line with two lines: `state.search = searchInput.value;` and `render();`.
4. Delete the old line `renderGrid(shows);` under the `renderGrid` function. The `render();` at the bottom draws the page now.

Try it: click **Drama**, then type **the**. Click Drama again to see all genres.
--- starter-append
// ---------- State: everything on the page that can change ----------
const state = {
  search: "",   // the text in the search box
  genre: "",    // the chosen genre ("" means all genres)
};

// ---------- Draw the page from the state ----------
function render() {
  let list = searchByName(shows, state.search);
  // TODO 1: if state.genre is not "", keep only the shows of that genre (getByGenre)

  renderGrid(list);
  renderGenres();
}

// One button (a "chip") per genre, in the box with the id "genres". The chosen one gets the class "active".
const genresBox = document.querySelector("#genres");
function renderGenres() {
  genresBox.replaceChildren();
  for (const genre of allGenres(shows)) {
    const chip = document.createElement("button");
    chip.className = "chip";
    chip.textContent = genre;
    if (genre === state.genre) {
      chip.classList.add("active");
    }
    chip.addEventListener("click", () => {
      // TODO 2: if state.genre is already this genre, set it to "" (all genres again),
      //         otherwise set it to this genre. Then draw the page again: render()
    });
    genresBox.append(chip);
  }
}

// ---------- Start: draw the page for the first time ----------
render();
--- hint
TODO 1 is an `if` that changes `list`: `list = getByGenre(list, state.genre);`. TODO 2 is an `if` / `else` that sets `state.genre`, followed by `render();`.
--- hint
TODO 1:
```js
  if (state.genre !== "") {
    list = getByGenre(list, state.genre);
  }
```
TODO 2:
```js
